 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6047+) **Capable early armies can organize themselves to capture Barbarian cities:** SASGameSummary and BBAI logging also found AIs with 9-14 units individually able (`UNITAI_ATTACK`) to attack and capture cities, but none assigned to organize the others (`UNITAI_ATTACK_CITY`) into a city-assault army, so this is a difference in `UNITAI` assignment, not their abilities. A K-Mod rule automatically broke down any group of 4 or more such units into smaller groups of 2 or 3 units if the group was in territory owned by the AI or one of its teammates. Because of this, many of the AI's military units could remain at home instead of grouping together to capture a reachable Barbarian city. To fix this, if no city the unit can reach within one turn needs it as a defender and the unit is not currently threatened, the AI now checks if enough attackers are available in the area, and if so, the AI assigns that unit as the expedition leader; the others can then group with it and they set out against a Barbarian city. For example, the save file 453 confirming run created four such expedition leaders. Among them, one was of a Spanish Chariot reassigned on T60 had gathered a four-unit group by T61, redirected toward Minoan on T73, and Spain captured it on T77. Another was of a War Elephant reassigned for the Holy Roman Empire on T79 was leading four units toward Khoisan by T86, and the Holy Roman Empire captured it on T90. See [KI#188](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#188---fixedimproved-major-base-advcivk-mod-bug-in-organizing-armies-against-barbarian-cities-could-park-65-82-of-an-ais-military-or-prevent-capable-attackers-from-forming-city-assault-expeditions).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6059+) **War preparations now take priority over Barbarian expeditions:** Base AdvCiv/K-Mod treated the period before a planned attack became ready as if no rival war were being prepared. For example, in save file 450, BBAI logging showed Hannibal preparing to attack nearby Willem, whose power was only 54% of Hannibal's, while 7 of Hannibal's units were still being managed for an expedition to the Barbarian city Scythian 22 tiles away instead of building enough strength for the much more profitable rival war. To fix this, the AI now uses normal war readiness as soon as it has any real war plan. In the confirming run, the affected groups of 6-9 units did not leave for distant Barbarian cities. Instead, they continued assembling enough strength for the planned rival war and remained nearby in case they were needed for defense. See [KI#188.4](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#1884---fixed-base-advcivk-mod-bug-ai-armies-preparing-war-against-a-civilization-could-still-be-classified-as-hunting-only-barbarian-cities).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6059+) **AIs no longer do charity Barbarian cleanup for their rivals:** Following the readiness fix above, BBAI logging exposed a broader problem: a fully prepared army could still spend many turns attacking a distant Barbarian city that it would not benefit from and ultimately raze. For example, in save file 450, BBAI logging showed that Hannibal mobilized 3 units on turn 74 against the Barbarian city Libyan, 16 tiles away. Reaching and capturing it took 16 turns, and Hannibal then razed it. He gained no city and only some conquest gold, but lost troops and time that could have supported offense or defense. Shaka, the only nearby player likely to keep the city, gained much more: he founded Bulawayo on the exact plot only 10 turns later without having to capture Libyan himself. With the nearby Barbarian pressure removed, Shaka could also focus on his economy or build up his army without losing units to Barbarian attacks or becoming easier prey for other rivals, and he eventually won this run by Space. To fix this, the AI now avoids trips longer than the new XML-tunable limit of 3 path turns (to preserve quick tactical raids even when the city is not worth keeping) unless `AI_isSASCityLikelyToBenefitUsLongTerm` says the city is worth keeping (as of now, generally because it is close enough and has enough useful land and food after bonuses are considered). In the confirming run, Hannibal did not send his units to clear those distant Barbarian cities around turn 74. Instead, he kept them available for potentially better attacks or defense. Much later, between turns 168 and 195, he captured 5 Arabian cities, which now made capturing and not razing Libyan and Scythian now worth it as they became close enough (and are otherwise valuable enough to keep), which he did on turns 204 and 208. As for Shaka, he spent his own early military effort fighting near Libyan but failed to capture it. He later became Hannibal's vassal, and Hannibal won by Space. See [KI#188.5](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#1885---improved-ai-armies-could-waste-their-strength-razing-distant-barbarian-cities-instead-of-leaving-barbarian-pressure-to-weaken-nearer-rivals-and-remaining-available-for-offense-or-defense).
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6063+) **Loaded assault ships can take achievable overseas targets instead of waiting for an army larger than they can carry:** Base AdvCiv/K-Mod generally required a loaded fleet to reach a generic army size before checking whether its actual units could capture a specific city by sea. For example, BBAI logging in save file 450 showed that a fleet consisting of one Galley (total Cargo Space: 3) waited for 13 units before carrying reinforcements to an overseas attack already under way, or 26 units before launching its own full invasion. This was a problem because the fleet could not reach either threshold unless more transports joined it; more generally, any fleet whose ships together could not carry its required generic army size could remain in port indefinitely, which was especially bad when nearby Barbarian island cities sometimes even had no defenders at all. This happened even though the AI already had a more relevant check that compares its loaded units' strength with the target's defenders and route, because the generic army-size gate could prevent that check from running. To fix this, once safety, escort and imminent-loading needs are handled, a smaller loaded fleet now also asks the target-specific check whether its loaded force is strong enough to attack a reachable target. Note: the normal thresholds remain for organizing larger invasions, and the fleet still waits when no suitable target exists. For Barbarian targets, it also avoids a long naval trip to a city it expects to raze, but a worthwhile isolated-island city is not rejected merely because it is on another landmass (as per `AI_isSASCityLikelyToBenefitUsLongTerm`). In the confirming save-file 450 run, 7 distinct fleets found valid below-threshold targets, while 76 other evaluations found no acceptable target and correctly kept their fleets waiting; the changed history produced 6 additional Barbarian-city conquests with none razed. Save file 449 independently confirmed the island behavior: India sent 3 units despite a threshold of 9 and captured the Barbarian island city Burgundian six turns later. See [KI#193](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#193---fixedimproved-base-advcivk-mod-generic-army-thresholds-could-make-loaded-assault-ships-wait-instead-of-taking-achievable-overseas-targets).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6049+) **Offensive armies can interrupt or divide an expedition to defend nearby endangered cities:** Following KI#188, similar logging showed a ready AI army, including one already in enemy territory after being backstabbed, could in Base AdvCiv/K-Mod select another enemy city while an underdefended own city was reachable within a few turns. The clearest diagnostic case was in save file 450: Baghdad needed 12 defenders and had 5, but a healthy 11-unit Arabian city-assault army only two turns away waited for more units, grew to 23 units / 32% of Arabia's military, and attacked Prague; Baghdad fell, Prague was not captured, and Arabia retook Baghdad five turns later after its population had fallen from 10 to 4. To fix and enhance this, before choosing its next offensive target, the army now first tries to counter a suitable nearby invader when its area is considered defensive. If no such counterattack is selected, it can send enough units to fill an endangered city's remaining defender shortage within three turns and continue the expedition with the rest. The AI does not begin a rescue when a one-unit group cannot fill a larger shortage or when filling it would consume more than three quarters of the group, and doomed-city evacuation still takes priority. This lets a military advantage serve defense without automatically abandoning every viable offensive. After the fix, history diverged, but a similar save file 450 run showed the intended choices working: an Arabian city-assault group suspended its expedition to counter Holy Roman units near Baghdad, which remained Arabian. Later, a Dutch army sent 10 of its 19 units to protect Amsterdam while keeping 9 in the field; the Dutch won three of four nearby land battles and successfully retained the city. Overall, 11 of the 16 expedition-split decisions targeted cities that survived the immediate war or remained held through the test. The five failures were all on the collapsing Holy Roman front, where Najran, Vienna, Nuremberg, Prague, and Aachen still fell, so the response improves viable defenses without pretending an overwhelmed empire can save every city. See [KI#188.2](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#1882---fixedimproved-base-advcivk-mod-issue-offensive-armies-could-ignore-nearby-endangered-cities-instead-of-going-back-to-try-to-help-defending-them).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6049+) **Emergency city reinforcements now complete their assigned rescue:** Similar logging showed that the first prototypes of the cure above could detach defenders only for them to immediately regroup with their offensive army instead of reaching the endangered city. For example, at Chichen Itza, 6 units detached from an 8-unit army, regrouped, and had to be detached again two turns later; the city fell before the rescue was completed. This was not an inherited Base AdvCiv/K-Mod issue, but a missing part of our candidate cure. To complete the cure, emergency reinforcements now retain their city assignment, count as incoming defenders so the AI does not duplicate the same response, and remain in the city while needed; once the danger or defender shortage ends, they return to normal duties. In the confirming save file 450 run, 7 units successfully reached Amsterdam while the other 11 remained together for their campaign. See [KI#188.2](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#1882---fixedimproved-base-advcivk-mod-issue-offensive-armies-could-ignore-nearby-endangered-cities-instead-of-going-back-to-try-to-help-defending-them).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6050+) **Large AI armies parked with nothing reachable to attack:** BBAI logging showed a major Base AdvCiv/K-Mod bug: even while at peace, with no planned war and no rival or Barbarian city it could reach, the AI could keep waiting for reinforcements and effectively lock more units into the same army so they could not be released for other duties. For example, in save file 450, a United States group grew from 6 units on T126 to 18 of the empire's 47 military units by T225, and then, after the short war, it returned to peace still holding 21 of 51 units on T247. So it concentrated about 40% of the AI's military in one city and left far fewer units available to defend the rest of its empire or pursue another opportunity, which was very problematic. To fix this, when the AI has nothing to attack, it now stops waiting for more reinforcements: if the army is already in its own territory and not immediately threatened, the AI now only keeps one modest core group ready for a future city attack. As for the excess units, they now instead leave the group and resume defending cities or preparing another expedition. Note: an army that is still abroad or in immediate danger remains together until it returns to its own territory and is no longer immediately threatened, so the fix does not break it apart while exposed. Note 2: the units can also regroup into a large army as soon as a real target or planned war appears. After the fix, confirming runs of save files 450 and 452 showed the intended behavior: in save file 452, Japan reduced its peacetime city-assault group with nothing to attack from 21 to 11 units, freeing 10 units for other duties; at the same time, the AI could still assemble large armies when a real war began, as Carthage attacked Susa with 63 units in save file 450, and China attacked Aksum with roughly 180-202 units in save file 452. See [KI#188.3](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#1883---fixedimproved-major-base-advcivk-mod-bug-city-assault-armies-without-a-reachable-target-or-war-plan-could-keep-waiting-for-reinforcements-indefinitely-and-park-40-53-of-an-ais-military).

